"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including the preoperative PSA of 2.4 and the pathological findings of Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) with documented extraprostatic extension and positive surgical margins. The absence of seminal vesicle invasion and lymphovascular invasion are favorable elements. A significant limitation is that no lymph nodes were removed, meaning nodal status cannot be assessed, which widens uncertainty. Furthermore, the biopsy findings (Gleason 3+3 in two sessions) differ from the prostatectomy grade (Gleason 3+4). The lack of postoperative PSA and follow-up data prevents further risk stratification regarding biochemical recurrence. Information regarding the trajectory of a subsequent postoperative PSA would be crucial for refining this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 62.79 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 37.67–87.91 months; its coverage has not been validated."
